sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch17-18> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In related fishes, expansion in one transverse dimension tends to be compensated by compression in the other, so that volume at a given length changes less than linear dimensions; the plaice is about twice as deep and half as thick as the haddock and has almost the same length-weight coefficient k = W/L^3.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Depth expansion and lateral compression vary inversely, conserving fish volume";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "plaice vs haddock: depth \\times 2, thickness \\times \\tfrac{1}{2}, k = W/L^3 nearly equal";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XVII, “On the Theory of Transformations, or the Comparison of Related Forms”; topic “Three-dimensional transformations of fishes”. Scope: related fish species differing in body depth and width (e.g. haddock and plaice, shark and skate)"
}
